        content: "Word processing tables represent another frequent source of prepress failure when migrating to CorelDRAW.\n\n### The Geometry of Imported Word Tables\nIn Microsoft Word, tables utilize dynamic cell padding, percentage-based column widths, and automatic cell merging. When exported to PDF and imported into CorelDRAW:\n- The table gridlines do not remain an interactive 'table object'; they convert into individual vector line paths or closed rectangular polygons.\n- Cell background fills convert into separate filled vector rectangles positioned behind the text.\n- The text inside each cell converts into an independent paragraph text frame or artistic text string.\n\n### Post-Import Table Optimization in CorelDRAW\n1. **Harmonize Stroke Weights:** Word often exports hairline borders ($0.05 \\text{ mm}$) that look fine on screen but break up or vanish on physical printing plates. Select the imported table lines and utilize the **Object Properties Docker** to assign a uniform stroke weight of at least **0.25 pt ($0.088 \\text{ mm}$)**.\n2. **Verify Cell Alignment and Snapping:** Activate CorelDRAW's **Dynamic Guides (`Alt+Shift+D`)** and **Snap to Objects (`Alt+Z`)** to verify that adjacent horizontal and vertical divider lines snap perfectly together at intersections without microscopic overlap gaps.\n3. **Ungrouping and Layering:** Imported tables are often imported as deeply nested Groups. Select the table group, press `Ctrl+U` to ungroup, and organize the background fill rectangles onto a dedicated background layer, locking it to prevent accidental movement while editing text.",
